ull,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in: v4.5.rc.custom_build [ee87ee833]\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.rc (ee87ee833) - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.8088) - AMD Ryzen 9 9900X 12-Core Processor (24 threads) - 61.66 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nThe current vswhere command in `methods.py` uses overly restrictive conditions that prevent detection of valid Visual Studio installations, causing build failures on some development environments.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Install Visual Studio Build Tools or a VS installation without the dedicated MSBuild component\n2. Run the following build command:\n    \n        scons platform=windows arch=x86_64 target=editor\n    \n3. Observe the following warnings despite having a functional VS environment:\n    \n        WARNING: Couldn't detect compiler version, skipping version checks. Build may fail if the compiler doesn't support C++17 fully.\n        WARNING: Visual Studio 2017 cannot specify a C-Standard.\n    \n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nThere is no MRP for this as it is a build tools issue.","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://